 ## Background
-Originating from the need to render complex volumetric data such as clouds and scalar fields in real-time applications, resolution adaptation techniques have evolved from static LOD selection to dynamic, time-driven adjustments. Early implementations in visualization toolkits like VTK focused on geometric simplification for unstructured grids [S1, unofficial]. In real-time graphics, particularly for volumetric clouds, techniques were adapted to include temporal upsampling, blue noise dithering, and frame splitting to handle performance constraints on varying hardware tiers [S2, unofficial].
+Resolution adaptation techniques have evolved from static LOD systems in traditional volume visualization to dynamic, training-aware methods in neural volume rendering (NeRF). Early implementations focused on time-driven LOD selection for interactive visualization of large scalar fields [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. More recent approaches integrate resolution adaptation into the training loop of neural radiance fields, using alpha masks and periodic upscaling to manage memory and compute [S1, unofficial]. In real-time graphics, dynamic resolution and frame splitting are employed to maintain frame rates in complex scenes like volumetric clouds [S2, unofficial].
